Subject: [PATCH 1/7] decoder: clamp slice allocation against forged array length

The decodeSlice fast path (msgpack.Decoder.DecodeSlice and the
[]interface{} branch of decodeInterface) called

    s := make([]interface{}, 0, n)

with n taken directly from the wire-format array16 / array32 length.
A hostile or truncated header (for example, array32 with length
0xFFFFFFFF) caused a multi-gigabyte up-front allocation before the
underlying short input failed, easily DoS-ing any service that
unmarshals untrusted MessagePack.

decodeSliceValue had a related bug: the test

    noLimit := d.flags&disableAllocLimitFlag != 1

was a literal comparison against 1, but disableAllocLimitFlag is
1 << 1 == 2. The result is that limits were never applied along the
reflective slice path either.

This patch:

  * Clamps the initial backing-array capacity in decodeSlice to
    sliceAllocLimit unless the caller has explicitly opted out via
    Decoder.DisableAllocLimit(true). Append still grows the slice as
    real elements arrive, so well-formed input round-trips correctly.

  * Replaces the literal-1 test in decodeSliceValue with a proper bit
    test, and lets growSliceValue cap each grow step. Hostile lengths
    can no longer skip the limit; well-formed input continues to grow
    as before.

Backwards compatible: same exported API, same wire format, same
results for non-pathological input.

Apply from the package root:

    patch -p1 < 0001-decoder-clamp-slice-allocation.patch

---
 decode_slice.go | 22 ++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/decode_slice.go
+++ b/decode_slice.go
@@ -101,14 +101,20 @@
 		v.Set(v.Slice(0, v.Cap()))
 	}
 
-	noLimit := d.flags&disableAllocLimitFlag != 1
+	// disableAllocLimitFlag is a bit flag (1 << 1 == 2); the previous
+	// comparison against the literal 1 meant the limit was effectively
+	// never applied along this path and a hostile array32 length could
+	// trick the decoder into requesting a multi-gigabyte slice up front.
+	// Use a bit test and let growSliceValue cap each grow step so real
+	// elements still flow through as input arrives.
+	noLimit := d.flags&disableAllocLimitFlag != 0
 
 	if noLimit && n > v.Len() {
 		v.Set(growSliceValue(v, n, noLimit))
 	}
 
 	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
-		if !noLimit && i >= v.Len() {
+		if i >= v.Len() {
 			v.Set(growSliceValue(v, n, noLimit))
 		}
 
@@ -170,7 +176,19 @@
 		return nil, nil
 	}
 
-	s := make([]interface{}, 0, n)
+	// Clamp the initial backing-array allocation so a hostile or
+	// truncated header (for example, array32 with length ~4G) cannot
+	// trick the decoder into requesting an arbitrarily large slice up
+	// front. The decoder still grows the slice via append as real
+	// elements arrive, so well-formed input with more than
+	// sliceAllocLimit elements continues to round-trip correctly when
+	// the limit is disabled via Decoder.DisableAllocLimit(true).
+	initCap := n
+	if d.flags&disableAllocLimitFlag == 0 && initCap > sliceAllocLimit {
+		initCap = sliceAllocLimit
+	}
+
+	s := make([]interface{}, 0, initCap)
 	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
 		v, err := d.decodeInterfaceCond()
 		if err != nil {
